Response (FULL, NOT TRUNCATED):
Thanks — I can help. First, a quick check: the numbers you gave contain a mismatch I should call out before drafting any final narrative.

Quick math and inconsistency
- Cash: $5.2M
- Burn: $195K / month
- Reported runway: 20 months

Calculation A (from cash / burn)
- 5,200,000 / 195,000 = 26.7 months of runway

Calculation B (if runway = 20 months)
- Implied burn = 5,200,000 / 20 = $260K / month

Calculation C (if burn = 195K and runway = 20 months)
- Implied available cash = 195,000 * 20 = $3.9M (not $5.2M)

So either (a) the stated 20-month runway uses a different definition of “available cash” (e.g., excludes restricted reserves, committed capex, or working capital buffers), or (b) the burn figure is being defined differently (for example, “operational burn” vs. “net cash burn” including debt service / one-time items). Please confirm which definition is being used before we finalize messaging.

Assumptions I’ll use below unless you instruct otherwise
- Burn = net cash burn (monthly cash outflows minus cash inflows from operations)
- Cash = total available cash (not including restricted or committed reserves) — but I’ll note both possibilities in the narrative.

Suggested finance narrative (ready-to-use paragraph + bullets)
Executive summary (one paragraph you can paste into a memo)
- As of today, Riverfront Logistics holds approximately $5.2M of cash. Over the last quarter we generated $3.2M of revenue (≈ $1.07M/month). At our current reported net cash burn of $195K/month, we have roughly 26.7 months of runway. If instead the organization is using a conservative runway definition that sets aside $1.3M of restricted/committed cash (or if the burn rate is higher at $260K/month), the available runway is ~20 months. We recommend clarifying the runway definition and presenting both “available” and “conservative” runway calculations to stakeholders.

Key points / bullets for slides
- Current cash: $5.2M
- Revenue (last quarter): $3.2M (≈ $1.07M/month)
- Net cash burn (stated): $195K/month → implied runway ≈ 26.7 months
- Conservative runway (if $1.3M reserved or burn = $260K): ≈ 20 months
- Recommendation: present both Base and Conservative scenarios; call out assumptions (restricted cash, one-time items, debt service, planned investments)

Illustrative scenario table (numbers to include)
- Base case (current): burn = $195K → runway = 26.7 months
- Conservative (+25% burn): burn ≈ $244K → runway ≈ 21.3 months
- Stress (+33% burn to $260K): burn = $260K → runway = 20 months
- Optimistic (-20% burn): burn ≈ $156K → runway ≈ 33.3 months

What to show visually
- 12–36 month cash balance chart under 3 scenarios (Base / Conservative / Stress)
- Monthly net burn line chart (last 12 months) to demonstrate trend and volatility
- Waterfall of one-time vs recurring items that impact cash (e.g., capex, debt payments, one-off settlements)

Recommendations / next steps (operational and message)
- Immediately confirm runway definition used in internal materials (what’s excluded from “cash”?)
- Reconcile last 6 months of actual monthly net cash flows to validate the $195K figure and show the trend
- Prepare a 12–24 month cash forecast with scenario sensitivity (±10/20/33% burn; revenue +/- 10/20%)
- Define an action plan tied to runway thresholds:
  - >24 months: continued growth investment with monthly monitoring
  - 18–24 months: restrict discretionary spend, accelerate receivables, extend payables where feasible
  - <18 months: evaluate financing options (debt/ equity / bridge), reduce non-core spend
- Report KPIs weekly to the executive team: cash balance, rolling 3-month burn, AR days, committed capex, and headcount hiring freeze triggers

Exact wording options for internal slide (short, medium, long)
- Short (one-liner): “Cash $5.2M; net burn $195K/mo → ~26.7 months runway. Conservative calculations excluding $1.3M in reserves / or assuming higher burn ($260K) yield ~20 months.”
- Medium (2–3 bullets): “• Cash: $5.2M • Quarterly revenue: $3.2M (~$1.07M/mo) • Net cash burn: $195K/mo → runway ~26.7 months. • Conservative runway (reserves or higher burn) = ~20 months — see assumptions.”
- Long (paragraph): use the Executive summary above.

Risk callouts to include
- Single-month revenue swings can move net burn materially given high revenue base vs. low burn
- One-offs (tax, legal, capex, acquisitions) can materially shorten runway if not disclosed
- Liquidity covenants and debt service timelines (if present) need to be flagged separately

If you want, I can:
- Draft a one-page memo or a short slide deck with these figures and 3 scenario charts
- Reconcile the exact runway using your runway definition (tell me if $5.2M excludes reserves / includes restricted cash / includes credit lines)
- Run sensitivity tables with revenue or burn assumptions you specify

Next step
Please confirm:
1) Which runway definition you want used (available cash vs conservative/excluded amounts) and whether any portion of the $5.2M is restricted/committed.
2) Whether the $195K burn is net of revenue and whether it’s an average over recent months or a projection.
3) If you’d like me to draft the memo or slides and which format (memo or 3–5 slide deck).

Once you confirm, I’ll produce the exact narrative and visuals ready for internal circulation.

