Riverfront Logistics enters the period with $5.2M of cash and an effective runway of ~20 months under current plan assumptions. This runway reflects an implied average monthly net cash outflow of approximately $260K, which preserves flexibility to execute planned growth investments while maintaining funding for near-term operating needs.

Cash flow and burn-trend details
- Opening cash: $5.2M.
- Implied monthly burn (plan basis): ~$260K; this includes operating expenses, targeted growth investments, and near-term capital commitments.
- Revenue last quarter: $3.2M (≈ $1.07M/month). The business is generating meaningful top-line cash conversion: planned net cash outflow represents ~24% of monthly revenue under the plan.
- Current plan-driven runway of 20 months provides a comfortable buffer to validate growth initiatives and pursue non-dilutive financing if necessary.

Implications and recommended focus areas
- Maintain disciplined expense control on discretionary spend tied to growth experiments to protect runway.
- Prioritize initiatives with high cash-conversion short-term benefits (pricing optimization, collections acceleration).
- Revisit forecast monthly burn monthly and lock in any planned one-time cash uses so runway remains accurate.
- Evaluate mezzanine or short-term working capital facilities only if strategic growth requires acceleration beyond internal cash.

B — Narrative using stated burn = $195K/month (resulting runway ≈ 26.7 months)
Executive summary
As of the reporting date Riverfront Logistics holds $5.2M of cash and is operating with an average net cash burn of $195K/month, providing an estimated runway of ~27 months. With last quarter revenue of $3.2M, the company currently demonstrates a favorable operating profile and ample liquidity to fund near- and medium-term objectives.

Cash flow and burn-trend details
- Cash: $5.2M; monthly burn: $195K → runway ≈ 26.7 months.
- Revenue last quarter: $3.2M (~$1.07M/month); current net cash outflow is ~18% of monthly revenue, indicating strong operating leverage.
- With nearly 27 months of runway at current burn, the business can continue to invest selectively, validate growth initiatives, and prepare for the next strategic financing milestone with limited urgency.

Implications and recommended focus areas
- Continue growth investments that demonstrate improving unit economics while monitoring burn-to-revenue ratio.
- Tighten cash collection and inventory/vendor terms to further extend runway and improve working capital.
- Model downside scenarios (5–15% revenue decline, 10–20% increase in costs) to understand how runway compresses and identify triggers for contingency plans.

Suggested scenario table (to include in the materials)
- Base: current burn; runway = calculated (use 195K → 26.7 months or 260K → 20 months depending on chosen figure).
- Downside: +20% monthly burn or −15% revenue → runway reduces to X months (I can produce exact numbers once we confirm assumptions).
- Upside: −15% burn or +10% revenue → runway extends to Y months.

Recommended KPIs and visuals for the deck
