- “Cash and runway: We currently hold $5.2M in cash. Our runway calculation is sensitive to the definition of ‘burn’ — if net cash burn is $195K/month we have ~26.7 months of runway; if the team’s 20-month runway assumption is used, the adjusted net burn is ~$260K/month. We should confirm which figure includes upcoming one-time commitments (capex, taxes, bonus accruals, or debt repayments).”

Drivers of the burn trend:
- “Stable operating burn: The $195K monthly burn reflects recurring operating costs (personnel, facilities, logistics ops).”
- “Planned / one-time items: The difference to $260K could be explained by planned investments (new systems, seasonally higher transport costs), ramp of hires, customer credit terms, or other one-time working capital outflows.”
- “Revenue context: Last quarter revenue was $3.2M. Current burn is a low-to-moderate percentage of revenue, but revenue volatility or contract timing could compress runway quickly.”

Implications & risks:
- “If revenue stays flat and burn remains at $195K, runway is comfortable (>2 years). If net burn is actually $260K (as the 20-month runway implies), runway is ~20 months and gives less cushion for unforeseen downside.”
- “Risks include unexpected customer churn, delayed receivables, seasonality, and large one-off capex or legal/settlement costs.”

Recommended actions (practical, prioritized):
- Short term (0–3 months)
  - Reconcile the burn/runway discrepancy immediately (CFO + accounting: include cash outflows for payroll timing, taxes, debt service, and one-offs).
  - Build a 12-month rolling cash flow (weekly or bi-weekly updates) with scenarios (base, downside, upside).
  - Freeze non-essential discretionary spend and re-evaluate hiring for non-critical roles.
- Medium term (3–9 months)
  - Prepare three scenarios: baseline (current burn), downside (20–30% higher burn or 10–20% revenue decline), and stretch (cost reduction of 10–20%).
